<!doctype html>
<!-- Generated once by nise; owned by this application. Nise will not overwrite it. -->
<html lang="en" data-theme="light">
	<head>
		<meta charset="utf-8" />
		<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
		<link rel="icon" href="%sveltekit.assets%/favicon.svg" />
		<!--
			Resolve the theme before the browser paints. `src/lib/theme.svelte.ts`
			owns the theme for the rest of the application's life, but a module
			cannot run before first paint, and the page background is painted from
			`data-theme` — so without this a reader who chose dark sees a white
			flash on every cold load.

			It is deliberately the smallest thing that removes that flash, and it
			duplicates exactly two facts from that module: the storage key and the
			three preference names. A test asserts the two still agree. Extend the
			module, never this.

			Its hash goes into the Content Security Policy automatically:
			internal/platform/webui reads every inline script out of the built
			document at startup and hands their hashes to the policy.
		-->
		<script>
			(function () {
				try {
					var stored = localStorage.getItem('theme');
					var preference =
						stored === 'light' || stored === 'dark' || stored === 'system' ? stored : 'system';
					var resolved =
						preference === 'system'
							? window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches
								? 'dark'
								: 'light'
							: preference;
					document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', resolved);
				} catch (error) {
					// Storage and matchMedia both throw in some privacy modes. The
					// static data-theme in <html> stays, which is a readable page.
				}
			})();
		</script>
		%sveltekit.head%
	</head>
	<!--
		data-theme above is always present and always resolved to "light" or
		"dark" — never "system", never absent. app.css keys both palettes and
		color-scheme off it, so the attribute is the whole theme mechanism.
		The static value here is only the pre-JavaScript default; the theme
		script replaces it before first paint.

		No literal style attribute may appear in this file or in a component.
		The Content Security Policy this application sends has no
		style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline', so an inline style attribute is
		blocked. Use a class or Svelte's style: directive, which compiles to
		CSSOM property writes that CSP does not govern.
	-->
	<body data-sveltekit-preload-data="hover">
		<div>%sveltekit.body%</div>
	</body>
</html>
